<em>MacRumors</em> videographer Dan Barbera got his <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-duo/">iPhone Duo</a> a day early, so he spent today answering some of the questions people are asking about the new device.<br/>
<br/>
<div class="center-wrap"><iframe title="iPhone Duo 24 Hours Later: Your Questions Answered" src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/7VfE8On97yk" width="560" height="315" frameborder="0" allowfullscreen="allowfullscreen"></iframe></div><br/>
On app optimization, some apps scale to fill the Duo's screen, and others have been updated for the actual Duo form factor with unique features for the different positions. Netflix has a special mode for a half-fold, for example.<br/>
<br/>
There are apps that haven't been updated at all, and those have thick black bars on the sides. The apps are usable, but it's not a good experience. We'll see a ton of app updates coming out starting tomorrow, so hopefully unoptimized apps become less of a problem. Multitasking with two apps side by side is useful, and it's nice to be able to do things like watch a video while scrolling a website.<br/>
<br/>
The &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj;'s camera is on par with an <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-17/">iPhone 17</a> camera. It's not as good as the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-18-pro/">iPhone 18 Pro</a> camera with a variable aperture, but it's not terrible. There is no telephoto lens, and camera quality is one of the big &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj; compromises. The under-display <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/mask-face-id/">Face ID</a> camera is okay for casual <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/facetime/">FaceTime</a> videos, but the quality is comparatively poor for anything else.<br/>
<br/>
The other feature people are going to miss is &zwnj;Face ID&zwnj;. <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/touch-id/">Touch ID</a> is not as quick as &zwnj;Face ID&zwnj;, especially because Apple <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/10/21/iphone-duo-touch-id-complaints/">built a deliberate pause</a> into it. You need to touch and hold to unlock. The Apple Watch can be used to unlock the &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj;, and that's an experience that's closer to &zwnj;Face ID&zwnj;.<br/>
<br/>
The inner display feels durable, and it's similar to an <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ipad/">iPad</a> with a nano-texture display. It does pick up fingerprints, but it doesn't feel like it's going to break or scratch easily.<br/>
<br/>
The &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj; is heavier than other iPhones, and it can feel bulky. Depending on hand size, you may be able to use it one-handed, though it's harder to use left-handed because the the &zwnj;Touch ID&zwnj; button and on-screen navigation bars are on the right.<br/>
<br/>
At a &#36;1,999 starting price, the Duo is expensive, and it has compromises that will deter some people. Once major apps get updated, the experience will change, and we'll have a better idea of whether it's worth getting.<br/>
